window.EidosReaderText["relatos-en"] = ["", "", "When time no longer has an end,\nit is the instant that teaches the value of the moment.\n\n\n\n\n\n     Felden Vareth", "Copyright © 2025 Felden Vareth\n                                           All rights reserved.\n   No part of this work may be reproduced, stored, or transmitted in any form or by any means,\n electronic, mechanical, photocopying, recording, or otherwise, without the express written permission\n                                         of the author.\n                                  Published on Amazon KDP.\n                       ISBN: 979-82-873-2664-7\n\n\n                      Cover design, layout, and editing: Felden Vareth\nThis is a work of fiction. Names, characters, places, and incidents are either the product of the author’s\n imagination or are used fictitiously. Any resemblance to actual persons, living or dead, places, or real\n                                   events is purely coincidental.\n\n\n                              web: www.eidoslibro.com", "To my family.", "ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\n\n\n   To  all those who,  in one way or another, have\naccompanied this journey.\n   To those who have shown me that humanity does not\nreside in what is grand, but in the details we so often\noverlook: a conversation, the light of an afternoon, the\nwind that reminds us we are still here. To those who taught\nme that every instant holds a singular value, and that our\ndecisions, even the smallest ones, reveal who we are.\n  My thanks to the readers, the silent guardians of this\nuniverse. To those who stepped into these pages willing to\nquestion, to feel, to think slowly. To those who chose to\npause before the nuances and to recognize in them the\ntrace of our own humanity.\n   This book is not merely a collection of stories. It is also\na pause, an invitation to look inward, to remember that\nshared memory, human bonds, and the way we transform\npain into meaning are an essential part of our existence,\nhere or in any possible world.\n   To my family and friends, for their constant support,\nfor  their  patience,  for  the  encouragement  to  keep\nsearching for answers. And for pushing me to write this\nsecond part. Thank you for teaching me to see beauty in\nthe everyday, and strength in the simple gestures that", "sustain a life.\n   To those who read fragments, scattered chapters, or\nideas  still taking shape. To those who offered time,\nattention, and a critical eye when there was not yet a\ncomplete story. Your companionship has also shaped\nthese pages.\n   And finally, to all those who remind me that life is a\ncontinuous process of transformation. That we discover\nourselves  through  small  decisions,  through  doubts,\nthrough  silences. Thank you  for reminding me  that\nhumanity often appears where we least expect it, and that\nit is there where we begin to understand where we are\ngoing and who we are.", "Synopsis\nWhatremainsofhumanity?\n\nAfter the Great Transfer, Eidos became both refuge and destination. Millions of\nconsciousnesses live there, sustained by a perfection that promises continuity and the\nabsence of pain. Natural death has faded away, yet the fractures remain, and no\nalgorithm can seal them completely.\n\nOn Earth, the Custodians continue their silent work. They interpret signals, rebuild\nstructures, observe and discover, with an unanticipated sensitivity, the value of what\nendures: the wind, the ruins, the life that persists within a condemned environment.\n\nEidos (Tales) brings together narratives that expand the original universe. Independent\nyet interwoven tales, in which scattered lives—a father divided between two bodies, priests\nwho hesitate at the threshold of the soul, families who did not reach the Transfer in time,\ngroups surviving in empty cities, Custodians exploring the world—reveal what remains\nof the human when the place to be born, to grow, and to die has been lost.\n\nEach tale illuminates a different facet of collapse and reconstruction: the ethics of identity,\nmemory as a boundary, the violence that emerges in the absence of law, the tenderness that\nendures even when everything is broken, the solitude of those who stayed behind, and that\nillusion of youth which does not fade with age, but wears away with time.\n\nThis volume explores existence, doubt, and our nature. It preserves the tone and aura of\nEidos and offers no definitive answers. Instead, it proposes a reflection on contemporary\nsociety, an invitation to contemplate, from both within and without, the fractures of the\nworld we inhabit and the human behaviors that sustain it.\n\nEidos (Stories) expands the universe of Eidos with narratives set before and after the\nGreat Transfer. It is an incomplete map of what was lost and of all that may still be\nremembered.\n\nAs in Eidos, this edition alternates between two color tones to distinguish events that\ntake place within Eidos from those occurring in the physical world.\n\nNote: This book contains references to and revelations about events from the novel\nEidos. ReadingEidospriortoEidos(Tales)isrecommended.", "Context of the Eidos Universe\n\nTheEarthdid not die all at once. It did so in silence. Like a person who no longer\nwakes  up, whose  breathing has  long  ceased  to  be  felt,  yet  never  quite  stops.\nFirst came the soils. Then the seas. After that, the atmosphere.\n\nCrops failed, migrations increased, tensions became unbearable, and the infrastructure\ndesigned for a vanished normality began to give way. The Earth ceased to be an option\nhumanity could still consider. Escape became an unavoidable necessity for the survival of\nthe species.\n\nThe Great Transfer was the  definitive solution. Human consciousness, with\nmemory, senses, and perception, with everything that defined us as individuals and as a\nspecies, was poured into a virtual environment built as a perfect and sustained replica of\nthe world. Death by old age ceased to exist. The intention, the desire, and the need behind\nthis new environment were to preserve the human soul in its purest form, to respect its\nessence. And that is why it was called Eidos.\n\nOn Earth, theCustodians, androids responsible for the servers and for maintaining\nthe systems where Eidos was housed, continued  to operate as machines without\nconsciousness. Until something began to change. In the silence of their labor, they began\nto observe. To pause before what was useless. To  feel curiosity and an unexpected\nfascination for that which they had not been programmed to understand.\n\n   The following stories delve into the lives of characters\nwho became trapped within this reality.", "Author’s Note\n\n\n   This book was born during the writing of Eidos. It was\nnot planned. It emerged at the margins, in scenes that\ncould not find their place within a narrative that needed to\nsustain an entire world.\n   The first novel required a broad, omniscient voice,\ncapable of constructing a system, establishing rules, and\nmaintaining  global coherence. These  texts asked  for\nsomething else. Closeness. Fragments. Specific moments\nin which the consequences of the Great Transfer filter into\ndaily life and affect intimate decisions, private bonds, and\nethical frameworks improvised along the way. Eidos\n(Stories) arises from that need, and also from the desire to\nopen a space that prepares the ground for the next volume.\n   Some stories linger on the moment of the Great\nTransfer  itself. Others arrive  just  after. The transfer\nappears as ceremony, as relief, as rupture. In The Family\nat the Threshold, the process works, yet the focus lies on\nwhat is left behind: the benevolent lie, the silent sacrifice,\nthe love that accepts not arriving whole.\n   Other texts move into anomaly. If two stories can\ncontinue, which one deserves to do so? The choice is never\nneutral. To follow one implies abandoning another. That\nfracture runs through several of these stories and admits\nno technical or legal answer. It is narrative.", "Rian embodies  that tension  in an intimate way.\nViolence and tenderness coexist in him, the urge to destroy\nand the impulse to protect. He holds on through routines,\nthrough minimal gestures, through an ethics he cannot\nalways articulate. His duality is his survival.\n   The Value of the Glasses was one of the first stories to\nemerge during the development of Eidos, and within it the\ndescent continues into the apparently trivial. An object\nwithout importance becomes a moral boundary and the\ntrigger for an entire life. Attachment to the minimal, to\nwhat is useless from any functional logic, reveals how our\ndecisions arise from silent bonds. That attachment, so\nnecessary for the development of the individual and\ncommon to many species, can become dangerous. It also\nmakes us deeply human.\n   The Ones Who Look Above does not seek to open a\ntheological debate. I was interested in observing two\npriests who, sharing the same desire to accompany others,\nmake different decisions when faced with a limit situation.\nIn that divergence appear doubt, the cost of choosing, and\nthe loss carried by every choice we make.\n   In A Concert for No One, loss comes from time. No\ntragedy  occurs.  Illusion  wears  away.  Even  in  an\nenvironment without pain or death, routine can postpone\nany attempt  at change  until no impulse remains to\ntransform it.\n   The Custodians’ stories observe from the outside.", "Earth remains a place where something irrepeatable still\noccurs: fragility. Reconstruction is born from curiosity,\nfrom questions that do not seek immediate utility. It is\nwithin those questions that meaning begins to appear.\n   Some texts include a layer of scientific dissemination\nand speculative thought, grounded in current debates, as\na framework for reflecting on  life, consciousness, and\nchange. Ideas such as the replicating molecule, the limits\nof language, and the difficulty of describing physical reality\nthrough mathematical models that may not fully represent\nit appear throughout. At times, ways of thinking about\nchange that lie beyond human intuition also emerge.\n   In other stories, such as those of Nori, the ethics of\ncreating a digital consciousness comes into view. It is a\nstep that has not yet been taken, yet it raises unavoidable\nquestions.  If we are the result of emergent chemical\nreactions, it becomes difficult to believe that we will not\neventually reproduce them. The question is what we will\nbe willing to assume when that happens.\n   In Transects in Eidos, the inequalities and the lack of\na collective project for humanity are revealed. Each story\nproposes an idea, or several, and leaves room for the\nexchange of opinions. It pushes questions that open onto\npsychology, philosophy, science,  ethics, and morality.\nThey do  not  seek  closed  conclusions.  They  invite\nconversation, shared and inward.\n   These stories, together with others in this volume, do", "not move toward a common conclusion. They brush\nagainst one another, intertwine, contradict each other, and\nsometimes ignore one another. As life does.\n    If this volume holds a unity,  it lies in a persistent\nquestion:\n   What deserves to be preserved?\n   What lies beyond?\n   What moves us today, and what should move us as\nhumans?\n\n\n   Eidos and Eidos (stories) do not ask what will happen\nif all of this comes to pass.\n   They pose something more uncomfortable: what are\nwe willingly sacrificing today, in the name of comfort,\nsecurity, and permanence?\n   The answer does not speak about the future. It speaks\nabout us, here and now.\n    Curiosity is born from contact with the unknown, not\nfrom absolute comfort.", "Índice\n\n\n\n\nSynopsis\n\nThe Family at the Threshold\n\nThe Guest\n\nThe Committee\n\nThe Story That Endures\n\nAgreement\n\nFile E-31/IECC\n\nThe Ones Who Look Above\n\nThe One Who Stays\n\nThe One Who Leaves\n\nKings of Nothing\n\nVehicles at Dawn\n\nThe Dark Night of Eidos\n\nCust-8431-ST/&\n\nThe Value of the Glasses\n\nThe Boundary of Language\n\nPersist\n\nA Concert for No One\n\nFirst Steps", "Rian\n\nAn Uncertain Origin\n\nThe Replicator Molecule\n\nEramus\n\nTransects in Eidos\n\nThe Committee’s Request\n\nFifteen Seconds\n\nPerímeter\n\nFreedom¿?\n\nThat is how we measure", "Tales", "The Family at the Threshold\n\n\n   Thomas set the glass down beside the sink. A ring of\nwater marked the countertop; Maya wiped it away almost\nwithout thinking and turned off the kitchen light. The\ndishes were still piled in the sink. In the dining room, a\nchair left slightly out of place held Irene’s coat over its\nback.\nIn the end, the house remained as it was.\n  A faint scent of lavender drifted from the bedroom.\nMaya closed the door without a word.\n   Evan tied his trainers in the hallway and, out of habit,\ncounted softly, one, two, three… Irene looked for Nala’s\nleash, stroked the pink collar, then clipped the metal ring,\nstill warm. The little dog sat with her tongue out, alert,\nwaiting.\n   They left the living room lamp on for one last look at\nthat room so  full of memories. One of Irene’s socks,\ncrumpled beside the sofa, was  still on the floor. Evan\nlooked at his hands, trembling, and shook them as he used", "to before an exam. The balcony window was left ajar, the\ncurtain moving with the warm morning air. Thomas, out\nof habit, closed the bathroom door so it would not bang.\n   Maya slipped her phone into the inner pocket of her\njacket and adjusted the bracelet on her wrist. The screen\nshowed a green code for a second.\n    “Let’s go,” Irene whispered. Nala obeyed, standing as\nsoon as she felt the leash tighten, taking a short step until\nshe was positioned at the apartment door.\n   Maya crouched to the girl’s height and pointed to the\nbracelet.\n   “We show it at the entrance and they register all four\nof us,” she told Irene, indicating her own bracelet, blinking\nblue.\n   The girl looked at her blue bracelet and nodded once.\n   They crossed the entryway slowly. Thomas took the\nkeys and approached the door, then stopped and slipped\nthem back into his pocket. He touched the wall beside the\nframe, where the plaster held a familiar crack, and his\nfingertips came away dusted white. He should have fixed\nit months ago, but it no longer seemed to matter.\n   The door was left just as it was, the lock unturned and\nthe latch loose. Maya watched the gesture in silence.\n   They stepped out onto the landing, leaving the door\najar, and waited for the lift rising from the ground floor\nwith a neighbour and a half-loaded shopping trolley.", "“They’ve already called us,” Maya said by way of\ngreeting, showing her illuminated bracelet. “I imagine\nyou’ll be in the next group. We’ll leave the door open in\ncase you need anything.”\n   The neighbour glanced at her own bracelet, still unlit,\nand thanked her with a faint smile.\n   The mirror of the lift reflected four figures and Nala, a\nyellow Labrador, sitting on the floor. Irene gripped the\nleash with both fists.\n   The building’s main door was left open behind them as\nwell. Neighbours from the block formed a calm current,\none after another. Green, orange and blue bracelets.\nArrows painted on the ground pointing toward the avenue.\nA volunteer in a vest directed people toward the stadium.\nMany shop shutters were half-lowered to protect the\ninteriors from the sun, and most doors stood open. The\nnewsstand still displayed newspapers from the day before.\nThomas glanced at them in passing and picked one up at\nrandom without stopping.\n   “For the wait,” he  said. “There are bound to be\ncrosswords.”\n   As they walked, a few cars and ambulances passed,\ncarrying people who were  ill or too weak for the walk,\nmany showing clear signs of radiation poisoning, thinning\nhair and sunken eyes. Among those moving on foot,\nsimilar blotches were visible on some faces and arms, the", "same that Maya already bore, and many showed a certain\nweakness in their stride.\n   There was no  sound  of  dragging  suitcases, no\nslamming doors or loaded engines. Just people walking in\nthe same direction, in a tense calm, with a silence that\nallowed their own footsteps to be heard and, from time to\ntime, a vehicle with sirens. Each family walked with only\nwhat they wore. Irene slowed for a few steps to wait for\nNala, who had stopped to sniff the kerb. With two quick\nbounds she returned to her mother’s side and adjusted her\npace to the family again. Evan moved to walk parallel with\nhis father. He was wearing headphones, which he removed\nand slipped into his pocket.\n   Thomas  checked  the  time on  his  bracelet. The\nappointment icon filled the screen with large numbers.\n   The access queue was a slow river, joined by people\nfrom different side streets, some of them blocked off by\nvolunteers. The closest entry point open to the main flow\nbegan at the end of their street. They merged without\nasking.\n  Among the families were a few pregnant women\nwearing double bracelets, an additional identifier that\ntechnicians checked more carefully.\n   The  line advanced a metre and stopped. A sign\ndisplayed bracelets by colour and age. Maya searched in\nthe distance for the registration area. Thomas bent toward", "Irene.\n   “Nala comes with us as far as the white door. Then a\ntechnician takes her so she can arrive earlier.”\n   Irene clenched her jaw and nodded.\n   The neighbour from the third floor was ahead of them\nwith her husband. He carried a mobile phone in his jacket\npocket, tuned to a radio broadcast. Recommendations\ncame through the speaker. She leaned closer to listen and\nhe raised the volume slightly.\n   The avenue’s surface bore fresh markings and paint,\narrows, numbers and letters. Evan followed a white line\nwith his eyes.\n   “From here to there in about three hours,” he said.\n   Volunteers and coordinators handed out parasols and\nbottles of water to those who asked. At intervals, diffusers\nreleased fine sprays of water to lower the temperature.\n   At  the  entrance  arch,  three  technicians checked\nbracelets. One line for verification, another for metal\nobjects, and a final security control similar to those at\nairports. The light  fell from above,  flat, reflecting off\nsuspended particles and giving that yellowish tone they\nhad already grown used to. The air was hot. Irene stroked\nNala’s neck and leaned close to her ear to say something\nonly the dog could hear.\n   Through a side access, a constant flow of buses arrived\nfrom nearby districts and towns. They entered slowly, one", "after another, unloading entire families who advanced in\nsilence toward the different access points. Once empty, the\nbuses turned toward the exit ramp and disappeared to\ncollect new passengers. The movement was continuous,\nalmost mechanical. The entire city and surrounding towns\nwere being relocated in coordinated waves.\n   The line wound forward toward the stadium arch. The\nbracelets flashed intermittently: green on adults’ wrists,\nblue on children’s, orange on the elderly. One volunteer\nmarked sections with tape on the ground. Another, tasked\nwith checking that codes were visible, passed Thomas\nholding a list and pointed to his wrist.\n   “Code visible, please.”\n   Thomas raised his arm. The green blinked and went\ndark. Maya took Evan’s hand. Irene showed hers without\nletting go of the leash. Nala looked at Irene, sensing the\ntension.\n   The automatic door opened and closed with a soft\nsound.\n  On the other side of the hall, a white tunnel marked the\nexit. Before that threshold stood the table for companion\nanimals. A technician in a coat waited seated, a notebook\non his knees, gloves folded in his pocket. Nala’s leash\ntightened again. Thomas looked at Maya, then at the girl.\nThree families still stood ahead of them. The day went on,\nand so did they.", "“Document and bracelet,” a technician asked as they\nreached the last control table.\n   Thomas presented his. A reader chimed briefly. Maya\nbrought her wrist closer and repeated the gesture. Evan\ncopied the movement stiffly. Irene raised her arm with the\nleash tangled in her other hand. The reader accepted all\nfour. The bracelets stopped blinking and held their colour\nsteady, showing the time and a black code.\n   “You need  to go  to the  pets  table,  please,” the\ntechnician said kindly upon noticing Nala. “Instructions to\nthe left.”\n  On the signs to the left, large lettering displayed those\ninstructions: “Do not stop.” “Hands visible.” “Place phones\nand other electronic devices in the trays. They are not\npermitted in the next section.” “Pregnant women, wait for\nmedical verification. The process will continue with the\npregnancy ongoing.” “Entry by family units.” “Pet owners\nproceed to the next table.”\n   In the background came the sobbing of children and\nparents’ murmured  lullabies, an  irregular music  of\ncomforting words, whispered promises, long breaths.\n   “There won’t be hospitals,” a father behind them told\nhis children. “Where we’re going, nothing hurts.”\n   Ahead of them, a woman placed a hand on her belly as\nshe listened to a technician’s instructions.\n   “The baby  will continue developing in Eidos,” he", "assured her. “It will be born in the new environment.”\n   She nodded, never removing her hand from her\nstomach.\n   “Always together,” a mother whispered to the girl she\nheld by the hand.\n  A woman cradled a boy of about four; the child was\ncrying, sores visible on his forearm.\n   “Where we’re going, the marks on your little arm will\ndisappear,” she promised.\n   Irene looked at her mother. That kind of phrase struck\nher as meant for younger children.\n    “Will Nala be there too?” she asked, lifting her chin\nwith a seriousness that made the question feel important.\n    “Yes,” Maya said. “She’ll go in earlier and wait for us at\nhome.”\n   The  rubber  flooring  throughout  the  installation\nmuffled  the  sound  of  footsteps.  In  the  distance,\ntechnicians gestured for no one to run and not to stop.\nFamilies advanced slowly and continuously, wearing the\nsame restrained expression.\n   Before the scanning tunnels, one  final table with\nanimal  pictograms  bore  a  sign: “Hand  over  your\ncompanion here.” Behind it stood a technician in a light\ncoat, a name stitched on the pocket: Nils Brenner.\n    “Is she yours?” he asked Irene gently.\n    “Yes. Her name is Nala.”", "“Very good. I’ll keep her for a moment. As soon as you\ncross, she’ll already be inside. She’ll be waiting at home.\nCan you let go?”\n   Irene hesitated for a second, looked at her mother, who\nnodded, and loosened her grip. Nala stood still, looking at\nthe girl. Nils accepted the leash with a patient gesture.\n    “Will she be scared?” Irene asked.\n   “No,” Nils replied. “She goes through another access, a\nshorter one. You’ll see her as soon as you arrive.”\n   Irene nodded. Maya, Thomas and Evan all knew Nala’s\ntrue future. At six years old, Irene was too young to\nunderstand. This would be the end of Nala.\n   Maya turned her gaze toward the instruction sign.\nThomas breathed deeply through his nose twice. Evan\nwatched Nala and his little sister: the dog, trusting, waiting\ncalmly; his sister happy because she would see her again\nsoon. Irene did not know that only her memories would\nrebuild Nala in Eidos. In the physical world, Nala was\ncondemned.\n   “A quick kiss?” Nils asked.\n   Thomas thanked Nils with a look for the care and\nhumanity he was showing in that moment. Nils responded\nwith a subtle, brief glance, half complicit, half procedural.\n   Irene wrapped her arms around the dog’s neck and\nrested her forehead there. She murmured more words that\nremained between them. Then she stepped back half a", "pace. With a gentle movement, Nils guided Nala behind\nthe side curtain. The dog followed, trusting.\n   In the vestibule, activity continued. Behind the curtain,\nNala  vanished  from  sight.  The  protocol  did  not\ncontemplate animal transfer. Records of scents, sounds\nand images were preserved: memories of walks, the\ntexture of her back, the collar, the clink of the tag against\nthe bowl. With that material, gathered from the family’s\nmemories,  the  system would  reconstruct  a  faithful\npresence. The merciful lie sustained the procession.\n   “When you reach  the  tunnel  belt,” a  supervisor\nannounced, “take a deep breath. It lasts a few seconds. If\nanyone feels dizzy, you may sit on the floor.”\n   Thomas squeezed  his  son’s hand. Maya adjusted\nIrene’s posture. The line stopped again. A cold, odourless\nbreath flowed from the tunnel. On the wall, a clock showed\nthe time in sync with the bracelets. Access barriers opened\nand closed with a soft murmur.\n   “To the line, please,” the supervisor indicated.\n   The line moved forward again. A volunteer raised a\nhand and pointed them toward the white tunnel on the\nright.\n   Thomas and Maya acknowledged with a nod.\n   Only the final scanner remained, a ten-metre corridor\nbefore the  last  arch. The tunnel exhaled an almost\nimperceptible breeze as they entered. The floor, a conveyor", "belt, carried them through that corridor of white light that\ncast no shadows.\n  On exiting, a sensor activated and opened access to the\nfootball pitch.\n   The stadium was dominated by two imposing satellite\ndishes aimed at the field, and a screen that once would\nhave shown the score, replays or advertisements now\ndisplayed a countdown: 07:05… 07:04.\n   Thousands of people waited there. Relief was visible on\nsome faces, sadness on others, and on almost all, concern.\nSome were sitting, others lying down, many standing.\n   They found space for the four of them near the goal\nclosest to their entry point. As they passed the final arch,\nthe clocks on their bracelets changed to show the same\ncountdown as the monitor: 05:31… 05:30… 05:29… 05:28,\nadvancing second by second toward 00:00.\n   Thomas stepped forward and embraced Maya. She\nreturned the embrace and placed Irene between them.\nEvan took his father’s hand and looked one last time\ntoward the white tunnel they had just left. The other nine\nwhite access tunnels were also releasing a constant flow of\npeople onto the pitch, who silently sought a comfortable\nplace, the location hardly mattering.\n  When the countdown reached 02:00, the white access\ndoors to the field closed.\n   “When you hear the signal, stay calm, close your eyes.", "Breathe deeply. Silence is best. If anyone feels dizzy and\nwishes to sit or lie down, you may do so. One minute until\nthe transfer,” the loudspeaker announced.\n   Thomas squeezed his son’s hand. Maya covered Irene’s\neyes with her palm. The girl held her mother’s fingers. All\nfour  bracelets  displayed,  in  perfect  synchrony,  the\ncountdown: 00:19… 00:18… 00:17…\n   Breathing could be heard; broken sobs; whispers of\nreassurance, parents trying to calm the crying of the\nyoungest.\n  A deep hum from the antennas filled the air and the\nbracelets vibrated. Thomas counted without sound. One…\nTwo… Three… Four… Five…\n   There was no longer any sound from the antennas.\n   Thomas opened his eyes first.\n  He checked that his family was safe, gathered beside\nhim. All four were in the same position they had adopted\nin the stadium, but now in front of their home. The door\najar, the living room lamp on, the coat on its usual chair,\nthe sock on the floor. Irene cried out and ran down the\nhallway. Nala waited in the hall beside the chair, alert, her\ntail beating the air in a rhythm of joy.\n   Maya looked at Thomas, they kissed and smiled. Evan\nglanced at his father and raised his eyebrows as if to say, it\nworked. Thomas called Nala. The air carried the scent of\nlavender, just as it had that morning. Through the window,", "the afternoon breeze moved the curtains, revealing a white\nlight and a blue sky Irene had never seen before.\n   They were in Eidos. Everything had worked.\n  … Four… Fi…\n   Thomas felt the pressure of his son’s hand lose all\nstrength, along with a weight that pulled him toward the\nground. The weight of Maya’s embrace also collapsed\nsuddenly. There were no more cries or murmurs or hum,\nonly the dull, almost simultaneous sound of thousands of\nobjects striking the ground.\n   And then he opened his eyes.\n   The scene was  infernal. Thousands of bodies lay\nscattered across the field, his family inert beside him.\nBracelet colours were visible at the ends of sleeves. Just\ncentimetres away, Maya lay on her side, her hand still\ncovering Irene’s face, the girl curled with her cheek against\nher forearm. All around him, everyone was dead. Evan lay\non his back, half fallen, his arm extended toward his\nfather’s, which he had not let go. The bracelet screens read\n00:00. In  less than three seconds, and without any\napparent order, all the bracelets powered down.\n  He was still standing.\n  He looked at his wrist. The green screen remained lit:\n−00:04… −00:05… −00:06. A second later, the screen\nflashed red.", "", "The Guest\n\n\n    “Sir,  do  not  move,”  a  voice  ordered  over  the\nloudspeakers, urgent. The emergency lights stained the\nstadium red.\n   Three technicians arrived at a quick trot. One took his\nradial pulse; another clipped a sensor to his earlobe; the\nthird pushed a cart of monitors. On the screen, the trace\nsprang to life: Oxygen, fine. Rhythm, irregular. Pupils,\nreactive.\n   “My family,” he shouted. “What’s happened to them?”\n   “Name?”\n   “Thomas. What happened? What happened to my\nfamily?”\n   “Stay calm. Your family is fine. They’re in Eidos. Chest\npain? Dizziness?”\n   “No.”\n  He looked again at his family’s bodies and those\naround them. The technician motioned for him to wait and\nspoke into the radio:", "“We’ve got a non-collapse. Send supervision.”\n   As one of the technicians fixed a sensor to his forearm,\nThomas caught movement beyond the circle of emergency\nlights. At the edge  of the  pitch,  several Custodians\nadvanced in parallel lines, pushing bodies toward open\nhatches beneath the stands. They moved them without\nlifting them, using broad plates of black material that slid\nover the grass like blunt, edge-less shovels.\n   To one side, another group was assembling a conveyor\nbelt in sections, slotting pieces together with precise\ngestures. The belt started with a low hum and began\nswallowing bodies into the dark mouth of the tunnel,\nwithout pauses, without jams.\n   From deep inside, through the tunnel opening, came\nthe heavy rumble of engines rising and falling with a\nsteady rhythm. Cold white work lights mixed with the red\nalarm lights and swept the field in bands. Thomas had the\nabsurd impression that the stadium was still operating,\nlike a processing line indifferent to what had happened,\nand that the only thing out of place was him, standing\nthere.\n   Director Livia Kern, a slight, middle-aged woman with\nthe air of someone used to being obeyed, burst in from the\nside corridor with a tablet pressed to her chest. The\nintercom  vibrated:  a  green  notification.  Arden  T.\nconsciousness validated in Eidos. She looked up at him.", "Thomas held her gaze without wavering.\n   “Thomas,” she said, “your record in Eidos is active, and\nhere you are still conscious. Do you understand me?”\n   “No.”\n   “I’m going to ask you very simple questions,” she\nadded, without raising her voice. “Do you know where you\nare?”\n   “At the transfer centre. In the stadium.”\n   “Good. Can you tell me who they are?”\n   She pointed to Maya, Evan, Irene. Thomas said their\nnames through tears.\n   “Don’t  worry,”  Livia  added,  checking  the  tablet.\n“They’re fine in Eidos… and you… are as well.”\n   Two notices were displayed. The first in green: Arden\nT: Eidos – Active. The second in red: Arden T: Locator –\nActive, blinking in time with the red on his bracelet.\n    Livia lifted her chin slightly. The technicians attached\nanother sensor to Thomas’s forearm and opened a new\nrecord. Thomas stared at the tablet and read, without\nanyone saying it aloud, the two words that fixed the scene\nin place: both active. He felt his breathing and pulse\naccelerate, thoughts and sensations crowding in, his heart\nracing. He knew he needed to take a deep breath, yet he\ncould not. He tried to think clearly, but each thought\ncollided with the next. I’m split, he thought, without fully\nunderstanding what it meant.", "His body and mind needed time to accept that the\ntransfer had divided him in two. His vision blurred, his\nlegs stopped responding, and a wave of dizziness forced\nhim to sit on the ground beside the still-warm bodies of his\nfamily so he would not fall.\n   “He’s going into shock,” one of the doctors said. “He’s\ngoing to lose consciousness.”\n  He managed to hear it before everything went dark.\n   Thomas woke on a stretcher, being pushed through\ncorridors by someone in a doctor’s or nurse’s coat, an IV\nbottle connected to his arm.\n  He tried to remember how he had gotten there, but the\nimages tangled with those of the stadium, and he reached\nfor his bracelet by reflex before realising he was  still\nwearing it, only it no longer meant anything.\n   Ahead, a technician opened doors with a card while\nanother took hurried notes. Behind them, Livia’s voice\ngave orders to reactivate the transfers.\n   “Review what happened and resume as soon as\npossible,” she said over the radio. “Adjust the alerts for\neveryone who hasn’t received notification yet. We can’t\nmove away from the schedule targets. It would mean\nleaving too many people behind.”\n   They reached a room that looked like a boardroom, two\ndark screens on the wall. Chairs were still out of place\naround a table, as  if a meeting had ended only hours", "earlier.\n    “I need your signature,” Livia said, already seated\nbeside him. They reclined the stretcher to sit him up a\nlittle. “Consent for non-invasive medical observation for\nforty-eight hours. Vital monitoring, brief interviews, rest.”\n   Thomas nodded without speaking.\n   “Your family is fine. They live with you in Eidos,” Livia\nadded, gentler now.\n   “He’s still in shock,” the doctor put in. “We’ve given\nhim a sedative.”\n   Thomas stared at the blank screen, as though he\nexpected it to turn on by itself.\n   “We’re asking you, for now, to remain in our facilities,”\nLivia continued. “It’s the safest option while we look for a\nsolution. You’ll have a comfortable room, access to the\ndining hall, and psychological support whenever you need\nit. An internal pass for authorised areas. No going outside\nuntil further notice.”\n   Thomas nodded again. A technician clipped another\nsensor to his earlobe and checked his pulse in silence.\n   “One more thing,” the director said, standing. “Your\ndog didn’t exit the circuit. They were going to route her\nthrough the animal protocol. I’ve asked them to bring her\nto you.”\n    It took him a second to understand.\n   “Nala… here?”", "“Yes. She’ll stay by your side. It will keep you from\nbeing alone. We’ll set up her bowl and a mat. Everything’s\nunderway.”\n   They opened the door. An orderly indicated the way to\nthe accommodation module.  Still with  the  IV, they\ntransferred him to a wheelchair.\n    If it hadn’t been for the circumstances, he might have\nthought he was in the room of a welcoming hotel, like the\nones  he  used  during  his  university  trips,  nothing\nluxurious, but practical and functional. The room was\nspacious and bright: a wide bed, a table with four chairs, a\nwardrobe, a bathroom larger than the one at home. Facing\nthe bed, a digital clock counted down to the final transfer:\n5 days, 23 hours, 48 minutes and 35 seconds, and a\ntelevision screen. Beneath  it, a console with a coffee\nmachine, a small fridge, a card with house rules, and a\nremote that also served as an intercom. A small balcony\nlooked out onto the exterior of the stadium.\n   Before he had finished sitting on the edge of the bed,\nthere was a knock. A technician appeared with a leash in\nhand. Nala came in without barking, sniffed the floor, then\nThomas, tail wagging, and sat beside the bed at his side.\nThomas rested his hand on her head.\n   “Thank you,” he said softly.\n    “Rest,” Livia asked from the doorway. “You’re  still\nunder sedation. We’ll see you several times a day. If you", "need anything, press the red button on the remote.”\n  When they were alone, Thomas set his signed copy of\nthe consent form on the table and lay down without taking\noff his shoes. Nala placed her front paws on the mattress\nand, after a brief hesitation, climbed onto the bed when\nThomas gave two gentle taps. She rested her muzzle near\nhis side. The dog’s breathing calmed him more than any of\nthe technicians’ words.\n  He did not know how long he had been unconscious,\nnor exactly how much time had passed since the incident.\nThey had not told him. But it must have been long enough\nto restart the system, because in the distance, over the\nloudspeakers, he heard the message again: “When you\nhear the signal, stay calm, close your eyes. Breathe deeply.\nSilence is best. If anyone feels dizzy and wishes to sit or lie\ndown, you may do so. One minute until the transfer.”\n   For a few seconds, before falling asleep, he thought he\nmight wake in the stadium again.", "", "The Committee\n\n\n   The  improvised  coordination room was  sparsely\ndecorated. Two screens displayed the AR-01 file; a table\nwith six chairs, only five of them occupied by the members\nof the crisis committee formed after the incident.\n    It was 23:00 on the day it happened.\n   Before sitting down, Livia asked Lena for a quick\nconfirmation that the protocol had been activated.\n   The facilities map appeared on the screen, with the\nstadium entry circuits and evacuation routes marked in\ngreen. Everything was active.\n  On the field, the Custodians advanced in lines, pushing\nbodies toward open hatches beneath the stands, just as\nThomas had seen through the emergency lights.\n   Beneath  the  stadium,  the  loading  tunnels  never\nstopped. Self-driving trucks waited with engines running.\nThey left full of bodies for old quarries, technical pits, and\nincinerators; they returned empty to repeat the route.\n    Livia nodded once and set the tablet face down on the", "table.\n    “I need executable alternatives today,” she said.\n   The legal lead, Nadia March, passed a sheet across.\n   “Legal framework: there are no precedents. There is no\nhigher authority to consult and no applicable case law.”\n   “Subject status: conscious, stable vitals. Good general\ncondition, high anxiety; still under sedation,” interjected\nDr. Félix Vorg.\n   The chief systems engineer, Lena Dorell, projected a\ndiagram, showing that no fault had been detected. To meet\ndeadlines, transfers had continued. The fault had not\nreproduced;  it could be one case in billions, and she\nconfirmed there were no recorded precedents anywhere in\nthe world.\n   “The  technical  record  is  clean,” Lena  continued.\n“There’s no evidence of error or of recurrence. If we force\na retransfer, we don’t know what will happen: the two\nminds might merge in Eidos; our Thomas’s mind could\neliminate the transferred Thomas’s mind, or the reverse;\nor… the death of both. In any of these cases it would be a\ncrime. They are now  different people with  different\nexperiences. If we retransfer into the same body, there is\nno prior experience to predict the outcome.”\n    Livia took notes without looking up.\n   “And  the  option  of  editing  or  partially  erasing\nThomas’s memory to remove  this experience?” Livia", "asked.\n    “Partial erasure might allow functionality,” Félix said,\n“but the subject would know his life has been cut short.\nThere’s  a  risk  of  chronic  ambiguous   grief  and\ndepersonalization. We’d be removing memories from a\nconsciousness without consent.”\n   James made an involuntary gesture, a brief grimace\nthat did not go unnoticed by Livia. The option was\nintolerable to him.\n   “And legally we would be liable,” Nadia added. “It\nharms continuity.”\n    Livia shook her head.\n   “Any intervention in his memories would alter his\npersonality,”  said James  Clive,  head  of  the  ethics\ndepartment at the center and the fifth member of the\ncommittee. “I know Thomas slightly; he was my tutor\nduring my university studies. He’s a good person. He\nwould refuse memory erasure, and frankly so would I.\nThat modification would change Thomas and turn him\ninto someone else. Similar, but different.”\n   “Without intervention,” Livia concluded, “erasing and\nimplanting  is eliminating one consciousness to create\nanother. It makes no sense.”\n    Félix raised a hand, cautiously.\n   “Another option is to keep him here. Explain that\nthere’s no possibility of a second transfer, that his mind", "has already been transferred and the process cannot be\nrepeated.”\n   The room fell silent at the suggestion. All eyes turned\nto the center director, who placed both hands on the table.\nShe was close to giving a blunt response, but in the end she\nsimply shook her head, restrained.\n   “There  is another alternative,” Lena said after an\nuncomfortable pause. “It requires global approval and\nisn’t simple. The system allows opening base protocols,\nbut we need unanimity across the network of centers.\nThere’s no time to debate options with them in just five\ndays. We need to present a bounded, viable proposal for a\nyes-or-no vote. They’re overwhelmed as well. If we give\nthem a clear, limited option, they can accept or reject it at\na glance.”\n   “What are you thinking, Lena?” Livia asked.\n    “Identity change, like a witness protection program.\nMr. Arden would have to accept it. It has consequences for\nhis life, but it preserves his consciousness. He would still\nbe himself.”\n   “Create a new avatar and transfer his consciousness\nthere,” she continued. “Legally there are nuances Nadia\nwill need to assess. Technically, it’s the cleanest solution.\nWe’d need network permission and Mr. Arden’s consent,\nwhich I don’t think he’ll have much choice but to give.”\n   “Nadia?” Livia asked.", "“I’d need to study it, but it seems the most logical.”\n   “Good,”  Livia  said. “We’ll go with  that  plan. By\ntomorrow at 07:00 I need the legal implications. Lena,\nfind the fastest way to issue a notice to each center.”\n   “Options on the  table,”  Livia enumerated. “One:\nretransfer with an uncertain outcome in Eidos. Two:\npartial editing. Three: no intervention and Mr. Arden’s\ndeath on Earth. Four: identity change. I’m choosing the\nfourth. Think of alternatives in case something better\nemerges; we’ll discuss them tomorrow. We need to request\npermission as concisely, quickly, and clearly as possible.”\n\n\n   At 07:00, the room was cold. On the screen: AR-01.\n  No one had slept enough; it showed on their faces.\n    Livia set the tablet on the table.\n    “Let’s go,” she said. “Alternatives and a plan that can\nwork today.”\n   Nadia opened her folder.\n   “No precedents, no  specific  legislation, no higher\nauthority. To modify a real event or a base protocol we\nneed approval from at least 75% of the centers, provided\nnone vote against. If a single center votes no, the protocol\nwill not open. We won’t get approvals in hours. Perhaps\ndays.”\n   Lena pointed to a diagram she had laid on the table and\nnodded, confirming Nadia’s statement.", "“All right,” Livia closed. “We have to move fast.”\n   Nadia was concise.\n    “I propose a framework agreement: compensation,\nidentity, employment, confidentiality, and a prohibition\non approaching or communicating, directly or indirectly,\nwith his family and former circles.”\n    Félix and James nodded slowly.\n   “That package is defensible. But the risk isn’t only\nexternal,” James said, looking at Livia as he laid out the\nargument. “It’s internal.”\n   Nadia didn’t wait.\n   “Next point: human risk. To protect him in the future,\nI propose absolute anonymity for his new identity. Once\nThomas chooses his persona, only the system will know it,\nand no one will ever be able to identify him. For all\npractical purposes he’ll have a new life, and only he will\nknow his true origin. To the network, we claim data\nprivacy: we communicate the exception and compliance,\nwithout the new identity.”\n   Lena raised an eyebrow.\n   “Technically feasible. Two-phase procedure. Generate\navatars and let him choose, or assign one randomly. There\nare minor movement risks until his mind adapts to the\nnew body. The system is calibrated for us to move and feel\nwith avatars identical to our own in Eidos, not others with\ndifferent physiognomies and weights.”", "Félix added:\n    “He’ll adapt quickly. The mind adapts. Still, it would be\nadvisable for the avatar to have a similar physiognomy and\nphysiology.”\n   Nadia held his gaze.\n   “Agreed, with informed consent. The fewer people who\nknow his true identity, the better. And any center staff who\nalready know it must sign confidentiality agreements.”\n   “And authorization from the centers?” Livia asked.\n   “We request approval only for the new avatar. No name\nor  traits,” Nadia  replied. “For  privacy, the  resulting\nidentity remains anonymous. Yes/no vote with a 48-hour\nlimit. With 75% affirmative, and if no one votes against\nbefore that deadline, the protocol opens.”\n    Félix closed his notebook.\n   “While responses come in, we prepare his local stay:\ninternal accommodation, a temporary fictitious identity\nfor staff who don’t need to know, a no-contact perimeter,\nand continuous clinical support. Short interviews, with\nme. The fewer who know his real name, the lower the risk\nof contact in Eidos.”\n    Livia summarized, counting on her fingers:\n   “One:    compensation,    identity,   employment,\nconfidentiality, and no contact. Two: generation of a new\nidentity unknown to us; confidentiality agreements for\nthose who know the real one, and a temporary fictitious", "identity. Three: circular to the network requesting the\nexception. Four: prepared stay while awaiting response.”\n   She gathered the tablet.\n   “Nadia, draft the agreement. Lena, the fastest channel\nfor the request; I’ll send you the note in an hour. Félix,\noversee the guest. James, go speak with our friend, see if\nhe remembers you. Don’t tell him anything yet. Tell him\nwe’re working on a solution and prepare a report on\npersonality and expected reactions. If consent arrives, I’ll\nexplain it to Mr. Arden myself.”\n   “Understood,” James said.\n   The others nodded in agreement.\n   The screen remained silent with AR-01.\n\n\n   Lena looked at Livia.\n   “Notify the entire network?”\n   “Send the message,” Livia confirmed.\n    “I’ve prepared a panel,” Lena said. “A map with each\ncenter. Grey: not received; yellow: received and pending;\nthey turn green or red when they vote. Each center sees the\nvote live. Ours is already green.”\n   As soon as the message was sent, all the grey points\nturned yellow. Seconds later, the first greens appeared.\n   “They must be as overwhelmed as we are,” Lena\nsmiled. “They’re voting yes, and it’s impossible they’ve had\ntime to study the proposal.”", "“There are many of them, and there’s still plenty of\ntime,” Livia said. “Let’s address the remaining issues and\nsee how this ends. The final forty-eight hours will be\nchaotic. In those last hours there won’t be appointments,\nand we’ll have to activate the center’s closure protocol after\nour transfer.”", "", "The Story That Endures\n\n\n   “Do we  die, or are we relocated?” James asked,\nabruptly, without preamble, as if continuing a discussion\nthey had been having for months. They were lying in bed,\nwrapped around each other, and James could feel the slow\nrhythm of her breathing.  It was a conversation they\nreturned to in moments of intimacy with increasing\nfrequency as the time of their own transfers approached.\nJames’s would be the penultimate one at that center;\nLena’s, together with Livia’s, would close the shift half an\nhour later with the rest of the technical team, in the final\nscheduled transfer before shutdown. Both wanted to make\nsure there were no errors.\n   “We’re relocated,” Lena replied, resting her forehead\nagainst his collarbone. “The system transfers, it doesn’t\ncopy. It preserves the pattern and continues it. The body\nfalls, yes, but what we are is not the support. It’s the\ndynamic running inside it.”\n   “The support dies,” James said. “That’s not a minor", "detail.”\n    “It isn’t, but it isn’t the core either. If you diverted a\nriver into another channel without losing  its current,\nwould you say the river has died, or that it’s the same river\nin a different bed?”\n   “Depends who you ask,” he smiled. “For the geologist,\na different fluvial system. For the child who played on the\nbank, that river has died. And the geologist would add that\nthe new chemical components change its nature.”\n   “Like any  decision we make,” Lena  replied.  “It\nconditions the circumstances and reshapes the nature.\nAnd the child… sometimes you have to travel farther to\nspeak to the same person.”\n   “And then Thomas appears,” James murmured. “Two\nrivers with the same source. Two stories claiming the same\norigin. If it were that clean, Thomas wouldn’t be here.”\n   Lena took a deep breath.\n   “Thomas  shows  that  uniqueness  is  a  practical\nassumption, not a law. Two continuities that don’t touch.\nIn Eidos, he’s still with his family. Here, Thomas is alive\nand looking at us. Which one  is the  ‘real’ one? Bad\nquestion. The continuity is double, even if it shares an\norigin. Choosing a ‘real’ one implies homicide and requires\neliminating one to safeguard the other.”\n   “And yet we decide,” James said. “We always protect\none story. We call ‘death’ an interruption of functions, ‘life’", "the continuity of a narrative. For us, whether we want it or\nnot, Thomas is this one; for his wife in Eidos, he’s the\nhusband who’s with her. And each Thomas recognizes\nhimself as the real one.”\n   “For me,  relocation,” Lena answered  after a few\nseconds,  returning  to  the   initial  argument,  “even\nrelocation without mourning. The Thomas there doesn’t\nnotice the fracture, suspects nothing. And our Thomas\ntakes comfort in knowing that, in some sense, his family is\nstill with him in Eidos. In the language of the body:\nbiological  death.  In  that  of  consciousness:  personal\ncontinuity. Two ways of naming the same event.”\n   James tightened the embrace and kissed her hair. Then\nhe said:\n    “Trial by fire. If every night, while we sleep, someone\nkilled your body and transferred your consciousness into\nan identical body and… you woke up without remembering\nit, with all your memories and intentions intact, would you\nstill be the same?”\n    “If there are no memory gaps, no breaks in my capacity\nto decide or in how I feel, I’d say yes,” Lena replied. “We\nare a chained process. The thread is the continuity of living\nconsciousness.”\n   “And if later they told you? ‘You’ve been transferred\nevery night  for ten  years.’ When does your answer\nchange?”", "“The story changes, not the process. My experience\nmight wobble. I might feel betrayed or angry. But the fact\ndoesn’t retroactively turn me into someone else. It makes\nme different now, when I incorporate the information.\nWhat hurts is knowing  it, narrating  it, putting  it into\nwords, not the fact itself.”\n   “Imagine this,” James said. “They’ve been modifying\nyou slowly, night after night, for years. Tomorrow they\noffer to undo all those changes and return you to the ‘you\nfrom before.’”\n    “I’d say no,” Lena  replied. “Going back wouldn’t\nrestore me. It would change me. It would be killing this\nLena to implant another with different memories. Maybe\nbetter, maybe worse, but it would be someone else. Every\nlife is the one that is remembered. I’d be replaced. If I’d\nbeen ‘killed’ every night to wake another, the one living\nnow is me. Reviving the one from then would require\nending this one.”\n   “Think about Alzheimer’s,” James said. “The person\nknows they’re changing: they forget names, leave notes,\nlook for shortcuts.”\n    “That’s change, not relocation,” Lena replied. “As long\nas they decide and  recognize themselves,  continuity\nremains. When decision breaks, continuity weakens. They\nknow they’re transforming and choose to continue. That\nknowing is also part of the self.”", "“And if someone accepted?” James asked.\n   “They would be accepting the death of their present self\nin favor of a reconstructed past that wouldn’t be them. If it\nwere  Alzheimer’s, recovering previous  capacities and\nmemories would  restore an  earlier  essence. But   if\nconsciousness is healthy, erasing this person to ‘return’ to\nanother unknown one  is nullifying a  life. That’s why\nThomas isn’t an argument. He’s two people, two conscious\ncontinuities claiming to go on. Choosing a ‘real’ one is\nkilling one story to save another.”\n   “So the difference between ‘dying’ and ‘being relocated’\nis partly semantic, partly ethical, and partly clinical,”\nJames said.\n   “And for a Catholic or a Muslim?” Lena asked. “Is\ndeath change or continuity?”\n   “Both,” James replied. “In the Catholic view, death is a\npassage. The soul remains the same person and answers\nfor its acts, but the horizon opens onto the absolute, the\nvision of God, and that transforms. In Islam, the soul\npasses through the barzakh, answers  for deeds and\nintention, and awaits resurrection and judgment. Identity\npersists,  even  as  knowledge  changes  the  person.\nContinuity of the self, yes, with new understanding of the\nworld.”\n   “Translated to our case,” James added, “if the transfer\nwants to respect those intuitions, it has to honor both", "continuity of consciousness and memory, and a new\nlucidity that makes the passage more inhabitable.”\n   “Imagine another test,” James proposed after a few\nseconds  of  silence. “The  transfer  bifurcates  as with\nThomas. One continuity ceases instantly and dies, without\nwarning and without time to feel it. The other wakes and\ncontinues in Eidos without gaps. Where is the person? Do\nthey die, or do they go on?”\n   “In the one that continues,” Lena replied. “Especially if\nthe one that continues is unaware of the loss. The criterion\nis effective continuity of consciousness. What matters is\nthe story that endures and the continuity that is lived.”\n    “That’s why we demand guarantees,” Lena concluded.\n“Two simultaneous  stories shouldn’t happen. They’re\nincompatible. If the second, whatever it is, never opens,\nthere’s no conflict. The one who can narrate themselves\ncontinues. The  system  is  designed  for  transfer  or\nrelocation of consciousness, not copying and death. The\nbody dies when it loses consciousness. The system doesn’t\nkill it to leave a single story. If the guarantees fail, what was\ntheory yesterday  is Mr. Arden today. And even then,\nthere’s proof the system doesn’t kill. For some reason, Mr.\nArden’s consciousness was copied, not transferred.”\n   “Thomas  isn’t an argument. He’s a man,” James\nnodded. “In his class he said the self is a promise we make\nto  ourselves upon  waking. Today  I’d  correct  him.", "Sometimes it’s a promise made in two rooms at once.”\n   “And you, what do you fear?” Lena asked, almost in a\nwhisper.\n    “I don’t fear the transfer, nor that one version of me\nmight die here,” he said. “I fear not being able to answer\nsomeone who tells me, ‘You’re not you.’ I fear the story that\ngives me continuity being hijacked by another who calls\nme a copy.”\n    “I know, because I’ve thought it myself,” he added\nmore softly. “It’s an idea I struggle to let go.”\n   “No one can take away your experience of being you,\nand that’s my job,” Lena replied. “Neither the process nor\nthe program will fail. You will be you. You’re the one who\nsigns your decisions, who remembers his mother, who\nloves. That’s what the system preserves. That’s why we’re\nworking on this solution.”\n   “And what do you fear?” James asked in the same tone,\nhis lips near her temple.\n    “I fear failing,” Lena said plainly. “I fear that through a\nmistake of mine or a contingency I didn’t foresee, someone\nmight get trapped between two lights. That what happened\nto Thomas might repeat, or that someone might not arrive\nproperly, because I would have killed them to create\nanother, different consciousness.”\n   She fell silent for a moment. James felt her fingers\ntighten slightly around his wrist.", "“Don’t carry that guilt alone,” James replied. “You’re\nnot the system. And if you want guilt, use this one: we all\naccepted living in a world that decided the continuity of\nthe pattern matters more than that of the support. We\nchose that vessel and now we sail in it, unable to fight what\nwas done to the planet.”\n   “And also because we already live a bit like this without\nrealizing it,” Lena said. “Every decision reconfigures who\nwe are. Even in sleep, the mind consolidates and orders\nideas and priorities. You wake a little different. The brain\nrewrites itself.”\n   “Suppose another situation in a different domain,”\nJames proposed. “Textbook teleportation. You destroy the\nbody here, resynthesize it there with the same pattern. Is\nit homicide with reconstruction, or is it travel?”\n    “If  the  reconstruction  maintains  continuity  of\ninformation, with no objective or subjective gaps,  it’s\ntravel,” Lena replied. “If any gap appears, then it’s no\nlonger exactly continuity. And that’s what worries me, that\nthe system leaves someone with gaps. Then it would be\nsuccession. And even then, succession sometimes suffices\nfor us to call something ‘mine.’ I trust that if succession\nappears  in  the  transfer,  people  will  still  recognize\nthemselves.”\n    “It’s beautiful. And dangerous,” James said. “With that\nargument you could justify too many things.”", "“That’s why you’re here,” she smiled. “To set limits on\nmy beautiful dangers.”\n   There was a silence. Outside, in the distance and\ndespite the late hour, the loudspeakers over the playing\nfield could still be heard: “When you hear the signal, stay\ncalm, close your eyes. Breathe deeply. Silence is best. If\nanyone feels dizzy and wishes to sit or lie down, you may\ndo so. One minute until the transfer.”\n    “If you knew that tonight, while you sleep, someone\nwill change your support without you knowing,” James\nsaid, “would you accept it with the same arguments as\ntoday?”\n    “Yes,” Lena replied. “I’d demand guarantees. That’s\nwhat we’re doing with the Great Transfer, and we’re all\naccepting it. We have no alternative.”\n   James ran his thumb over Lena’s wrist, where the\nbracelet pulsed along with her heartbeat.\n   “I’m the penultimate,” he said. “You’re the last. I’ll wait\nfor you on the other side.”\n    “If one day I wake up and someone tells me I’ve been\ntransferred every night,” Lena replied, “I’ll ask you the\nsame thing I’m asking now. Look at me and tell me if I’m\nstill me.”\n   James did it then, as a rehearsal.\n    “It’s you,” he said. “Even if you change rivers. Even if\nthe channel changes us. It’s you because you’re still the one", "who looks at me and understands why I’m saying this.”\n   “And you’re you because you never stop questioning\neverything,” Lena said with a smile James could feel in her\ntone and in the gesture, though he didn’t see it.\n   The initial question  felt lighter as  it drifted away.\nBefore falling asleep, James searched for her hand and she\nrested her head on his chest. They said nothing more. They\nsimply drew a little closer.", "", "Agreement\n\n\n   “Case AR-01. We’re starting,” Livia said. “This is the\nlast meeting on the Thomas case. We have permission to\ngenerate a new identity. Seventy-seven point five seven\npercent of votes were in favor; the rest did not respond.\nI’ve  received  emails  from  several  directors. Some\nexpressed  doubts. Some  of those  are  green. Others\nabstained. I’m aware many didn’t even read the message.\nWhat matters is that we have access to the code to generate\na new avatar and transfer Mr. Arden’s consciousness into\nit.”\n   “How do we tell him?” Nadia asked.\n   “Given what he’s been through, and knowing him\nbetter now, having spoken with him these past two days,\nand now with a more detailed psychological profile, I think\nhe’ll understand the situation. It’s a pity he hasn’t been\nable to take part in these meetings, for obvious reasons,\nbecause his input would have been valuable if it weren’t his\ncase,” Félix added, looking at Nadia and James.", "Livia looked at James.\n    “I completely agree with Félix. I’ve spoken with him as\nwell. He’s aware of his limited options. He’s very rational.\nI think he’ll accept. He understands the dilemma his\npresence here creates, and that in some sense he’s already\nin Eidos, at least the version of him from two days ago.”\n   Nadia tapped her tablet and a figure appeared on the\nroom screen. Everyone was taken aback by the amount.\n    “Triple it,” Livia said.\n    “But, Livia, that amount is more than enough,” Nadia\nreplied. “It’s based on real calculations and a generous\nsettlement in case he had sued us. In fact, a very good\nsettlement.”\n   “We stole this man’s life,” Livia replied. “It could have\nhappened to any of us. Lena, if there are no restrictions on\ncompensation, speak to Systems and have them assign\nhim a good home and set up an account with triple that\namount.”\n   “They haven’t set limits,” Lena said. “We have a free\nhand to have the avatar arrive in Eidos however we want.”\n    “Technically, we haven’t stolen anything from him,”\nNadia objected.\n   “This man’s life has been stolen from him,” Livia said,\nfinal. “Speak to Systems and fix it for him. I’m going to talk\nto Thomas and see if he accepts. He’d better accept. It’s his\nonly way out.”", "Livia sat in one of the four chairs in Thomas’s room, set\nher tablet on the table, kept a closed folder in her lap, and\nhanded him another tablet, powered off, along with a\ndocument to sign. Thomas signed with a pen: “Discharge\nfrom non-invasive medical observation for forty-eight\nhours.” Livia waited as he replaced the cap.\n   “Your family is fine. They live with you in Eidos,” she\nsaid gently. It was the first time they had seen each other\nsince the incident.\n   Thomas looked up.\n   “Then… they’re okay?”\n    “Yes. They’re moving forward there. You’re moving\nforward here too.”\n  He looked at his hands. They weren’t trembling. In the\nsilence, the ventilation’s low murmur could be heard. Livia\nopened the folder.\n   “You are the only recorded case. The technicians have\nreviewed  everything and found  nothing  that  failed.\nTransfers have continued without incident. We consulted\nother centers. There are no precedents anywhere in the\nworld. We  evaluated  alternatives and  see  only one\noperational solution. We have created a process, the only\none in the world, for you, to give you an exit.”\n   Thomas waited.", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "This is just the beginning...\n\n    If this story sparked your imagination, stirred your\nemotions, or made you question something, remember —\nthis is just a small glimpse.\n   The full novel awaits you, ready to take you even\ndeeper into the universe of Eidos — its dilemmas, its\ncharacters, and the essential questions about what  it\nmeans to be alive.\n   You can get the complete book here:\nAMAZON.COM/EIDOS-BOOK\n   And if, once you've finished it, you feel\nlike sharing what it left you with, your\nreview on Amazon can make a  real\ndifference.\n    It not only helps other readers discover\nthis story — it also supports the work of\nindependent authors like myself.\n   You can leave your review here:\nAMAZON.COM/REVIEW/EIDOS\nThank you for reading,\nFelden Vareth"];
